{"name":"Rice Paddy","hex":"#dfd4b0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rice paddy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A paddy field where rice is grown."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rice_paddy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Paddy field","blocks":[{"text":"A paddy field is a flooded field of arable land used for growing semiaquatic crops, most notably rice and taro. It originates from the Neolithic rice-farming cultures of the Yangtze River basin in southern China, associated with pre-Austronesian and Hmong-Mien cultures. It was spread in prehistoric times by the expansion of Austronesian peoples to Island Southeast Asia, Madagascar, Melanesia, Micronesia, and Polynesia. The technology was acquired by other cultures in mainland Asia for rice farming, spreading to East Asia, Mainland Southeast Asia, and South Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Pana_Banaue_Rice_Terraces_%28Cropped%29.jpg/330px-Pana_Banaue_Rice_Terraces_%28Cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paddy_field"},{"title":"Rice","blocks":[{"text":"Rice is a cereal grain and, in its domesticated form, is the staple food of over half of the world's population, particularly in Africa and Asia. Rice is the seed of the grass species Oryza sativa —or, less commonly, Oryza glaberrima. Asian rice was domesticated in China some 13,500 to 8,200 years ago; African rice was domesticated independently in West Africa about 3,000 years ago. Rice has become commonplace in many cultures worldwide; in 2023, 800 million tons were produced, placing it third after sugarcane and maize. Only some 8% of rice is traded internationally. China, India, and Indonesia are the largest consumers of rice. A substantial amount of the rice produced in developing nations is lost after harvest through factors such as poor transport and storage. Rice yields can be reduced by pests including insects, rodents, and birds, as well as by weeds, and by diseases such as rice blast. Traditional rice polycultures such as rice-duck farming, and modern integrated pest management seek to control damage from pests in a sustainable way."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/20201102.Hengnan.Hybrid_rice_Sanyou-1.6.jpg/330px-20201102.Hengnan.Hybrid_rice_Sanyou-1.6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Model_of_Liangzhu_Ancient_City.jpg/500px-Model_of_Liangzhu_Ancient_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Model_of_Liangzhu_Ancient_City.jpg/1280px-Model_of_Liangzhu_Ancient_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Model_of_Liangzhu_Ancient_City.jpg/500px-Model_of_Liangzhu_Ancient_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Model of a Liangzhu culture (3400 to 2250 BC) ancient city surrounded by a moat with rice paddies","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AModel_of_Liangzhu_Ancient_City.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Spatial_distribution_of_rice%2C_millet_and_mixed_farming_sites_with_a_boundary_of_rice_and_millet_and_possible_centers_of_agriculture.png/500px-Spatial_distribution_of_rice%2C_millet_and_mixed_farming_sites_with_a_boundary_of_rice_and_millet_and_possible_centers_of_agriculture.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Spatial_distribution_of_rice%2C_millet_and_mixed_farming_sites_with_a_boundary_of_rice_and_millet_and_possible_centers_of_agriculture.png/1280px-Spatial_dist